        passageEn:
            "For the Term 2 capstone, Nova School's computing class held a Robot Challenge: each pair had to write a sequence of commands to guide a small floor robot through a maze drawn on the classroom carpet, using only \"forward,\" \"turn left,\" and \"turn right.\"\n\nThiha and Zin Min wrote their sequence carefully on paper first, counting each square of the maze and matching it to a precise number of \"forward\" commands. When they tested it, the robot took a wrong turn at the third corner and bumped into the maze wall.\n\nInstead of panicking, they debugged the sequence step by step, comparing each command against the maze on the carpet, and found that they had written \"turn right\" where they meant \"turn left.\" After fixing the single sequence error, the robot completed the entire maze perfectly on their next attempt.\n\nMs. Thanda told the class that this was exactly what algorithmic thinking looked like in action: breaking a big problem into a precise sequence, testing it, and calmly debugging any mistakes -- the same process Thiha had already practised with his tea algorithm and flowchart.",
