        passageEn:
            "Thet Htar's final Term 2 project was a console-based contact book, combining everything she had learned about lists, dictionaries, file handling, and CSV parsing into one working CRUD system. Her contacts were stored in \"contacts.csv\", one row per person: name, phone, email. When her program started, it read every row from the file using the csv module and loaded them into a list of dictionaries in memory, so they were fast to search and display without touching the file constantly. Her menu-driven interface printed four numbered choices every time it ran: 1) Create a new contact, 2) Read (search or list) contacts, 3) Update an existing contact, 4) Delete a contact, and 5) Exit. Choosing Create asked for a name, phone, and email, added a new dictionary to the in-memory list, and immediately appended a matching row to \"contacts.csv\" using append mode, so the new contact wouldn't be lost even if the program crashed a moment later. Choosing Update searched the list for a matching name, changed the requested field, and then rewrote the entire CSV file in write mode with every contact's current data, since there was no way to edit just one line inside an existing file directly. Choosing Delete removed the matching dictionary from the list and, just like Update, rewrote the whole file afterward, so the deleted person's row genuinely disappeared from \"contacts.csv\" and not just from memory. Thet Htar's teacher noted that this project was a genuine milestone: everything from Weeks 5 to 7 -- dictionaries, persistent file storage, and CSV parsing -- had come together into one real, working piece of software.",
