        passageEn:
            "Moe Moe was asked to design a database for her school, and her first instinct was to build one giant table with every student's name, their teacher's name, their teacher's subject, and their class, all repeated on every single row. Her teacher pointed out the problem: if a teacher's subject changed, Moe Moe would have to find and update it on dozens of rows, one for every student in that teacher's class, and if she missed even one row, the data would become inconsistent. Instead, Moe Moe designed three separate entities, each with its own table: Students (StudentID, Name), Teachers (TeacherID, Name, Subject), and Classes (ClassID, TeacherID). StudentID and TeacherID were each a primary key, uniquely identifying one row in their own table. The Classes table's TeacherID column was a foreign key, referring back to the Teachers table rather than repeating the teacher's name and subject directly. Now, if a teacher's subject changed, Moe Moe only had to update one single row in the Teachers table, and every class linked to that teacher via the foreign key relationship would automatically reflect the correct subject the next time the tables were joined together. Moe Moe realized that normalizing her design this way -- splitting one repetitive giant table into several smaller, linked tables -- meant every piece of data lived in exactly one place, and relationships between entities were expressed cleanly through primary and foreign keys instead of being duplicated everywhere.",
