            "Aung Aung had designed a Books table on paper back in Week 13, but until now it existed only as a plan. To make it real, he wrote import sqlite3 at the top of his program, then conn = sqlite3.connect(\"library.db\") -- this single line created a new database file if \"library.db\" didn't already exist, or opened the existing one if it did. From that connection, he created cursor = conn.cursor(), the object he would use to actually run SQL. He first ran cursor.execute(\"CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS Books (BookID INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, Title TEXT)\") to build the table's structure. To add a book, he ran cursor.execute(\"INSERT INTO Books VALUES (1, 'Python Basics')\"), followed immediately by conn.commit() -- without that commit, Aung Aung learned the hard way, his insert would vanish the moment the program closed, since commit() is what actually writes pending changes to the file on disk. To check his work, he ran cursor.execute(\"SELECT * FROM Books\") and then rows = cursor.fetchall(), storing every matching row as a Python list he could loop through and print. When he only wanted a single result, like checking one specific BookID, he used cursor.fetchone() instead, which returned just the next row rather than the whole list. By the end of the week, Aung Aung's \"library.db\" file held real book records that survived every time he closed and reopened his program -- his Week 13 database design was no longer just a plan, but a genuine, working database driven entirely by Python code.",
