        passageEn:
            "Thiha wanted to launch a small website for his school's computing club, and along the way he encountered four different protocols, each doing a completely different job. First, to actually build and upload his website's files -- HTML pages, images, and stylesheets -- onto a remote web server, he used FTP, a protocol designed specifically for transferring files between his computer (the client) and the server. Once his site was live, visitors' browsers loaded it using HTTP, the protocol that requests and delivers web pages. But Thiha's teacher insisted the club's sign-up form, which collected students' names and emails, use HTTPS instead of plain HTTP, since HTTPS encrypts data in transit -- without it, someone intercepting the connection could read every sign-up submitted in plain, readable text. Finally, when a student submitted the sign-up form, the website automatically sent Thiha a confirmation email, which travelled between mail servers using SMTP, the protocol dedicated to routing email from sender to recipient. Underneath all four of these protocols, TCP/IP quietly did its job the whole time: breaking every request and response into small addressed packets, routing them across the internet, and reassembling them correctly at the other end, regardless of whether the data on top was an FTP file transfer, an HTTP page request, an HTTPS encrypted form, or an SMTP email. Thiha realized that a single website launch actually depended on an entire stack of protocols, each specialized for one job, all built on the same underlying TCP/IP foundation.",
