        passageEn:
            "Pyae Phyo had written a Python function, grade_from_score(score), that converted a score from 0 to 100 into a letter grade, and his teacher asked him to test it thoroughly before submitting it. He started with black-box testing: without looking at his own code's internal logic, he simply called the function with different inputs and checked whether the outputs matched what he expected, treating the function as a sealed box. For normal data, he tried score = 75, expecting a middle-range grade, and confirmed it worked. For boundary data, he tried score = 0 and score = 100, the smallest and largest valid scores, since bugs often hide exactly at these edges -- and indeed, he discovered his function incorrectly labelled a perfect 100 as an error because of an off-by-one mistake in his comparison. For erroneous data, he tried score = -10 and score = \"ninety\", values that should never really occur but that a careless user or a bug elsewhere in the program might still pass in; his function crashed on both, revealing it needed proper input validation, echoing exactly what he had learned about try/except back in Term 1. Once Pyae Phyo had fixed these issues using black-box testing alone, he switched to white-box testing: this time he opened his own code and traced through every branch of his if/elif logic by hand, confirming each condition was reachable and correctly ordered, catching one more subtle bug where two conditions overlapped in a way that black-box testing with his chosen inputs had not happened to reveal. He organized all of these checks into a proper unit test -- a small, automated piece of code that called grade_from_score() with each test value and automatically compared the result to the expected answer, so he could rerun every single test in seconds any time he changed the function later, rather than manually retyping each check by hand.",
