            "Sandar's class ran a survey asking every student how many hours of sleep they got the night before an exam, and she volunteered to analyze the results in Python. The raw survey data, however, was messy: a few students had left the question blank, one had typed \"eight\" instead of the number 8, and one entry read 80, almost certainly a typo for 8. Before calculating anything, Sandar cleaned the data: she removed the blank entries rather than guessing a value, corrected \"eight\" to 8 since it clearly meant the same thing, and flagged 80 as an obvious outlier -- no student sleeps 80 hours -- correcting it to 8 as well, since it matched a pattern of accidentally typing an extra zero. Only after this cleaning did Sandar calculate her statistical summary: a mean of 6.8 hours, a median of 7 hours, a minimum of 4, and a maximum of 9. She noted that if she had calculated the mean using the uncleaned data, the impossible 80-hour entry alone would have dragged the average up to a wildly misleading number, proof that skipping data cleaning can silently corrupt every statistic calculated afterward. Finally, Sandar turned her cleaned data into a bar chart showing how many students slept each number of hours, and the pattern immediately jumped out visually -- a clear cluster around 6-8 hours -- in a way that would have been much harder to notice by scanning a plain list of thirty numbers. Sandar's teacher praised the order she had followed: clean first, summarize second, visualize third, since skipping straight to a chart or a statistic on dirty data would have produced a confident-looking but genuinely wrong result.",
