      xpReward: 10,
      readingPassage: ReadingPassageModel(
        titleEn: "Thet Su Finds Bias in a Class Project",
        titleMy: "Thet Su Class Project တစ်ခုတွင် Bias ကို တွေ့ရှိခြင်း",
        passageEn:
            "Thet Su's group built a simple supervised learning model, from Week 29's foundations, to predict which students were likely to need extra help based on past students' data. Their training dataset came entirely from one specific class from two years ago, since it was the only labeled data readily available. When Thet Su tested the finished model on her own, more diverse class, she noticed something troubling: it consistently flagged students from certain backgrounds as \"likely to need extra help\" far more often than seemed reasonable, even for students who were doing perfectly well. She realized the model had learned algorithmic bias -- not because anyone had intentionally programmed it to discriminate, but because the training data itself reflected patterns from a class that, for unrelated historical reasons, had an uneven mix of students, and the model had faithfully learned and repeated that skew rather than anything genuinely predictive. Thet Su also raised a second concern: the training data included students' full names and personal details without ever asking those students for permission to use their information this way, a clear data privacy problem regardless of the model's accuracy. When she brought both issues to her teacher, the class discussed AI accountability directly: if this model had actually been used to make real decisions about real students, who would be responsible for the unfair outcomes -- the students who originally collected the data, the team who built the model, or the school that deployed it? The class agreed there was no single easy answer, but that responsibility couldn't simply disappear just because a computer, rather than a person, had made the biased call. Thet Su's group ultimately retrained their model on a much more diverse dataset, collected with the informed consent of the students involved, and treated its predictions as a helpful suggestion for a teacher to double-check, never as an automatic, unquestioned decision.",
