            "Wai Yan's final Term 8 project asked his group to build a supervised learning model predicting whether a student would need extra tutoring, and to evaluate it responsibly from start to finish. They began exactly as Week 30 had taught: cleaning their raw dataset by removing duplicate student records, fixing typos, and filling in a handful of missing attendance values rather than leaving them blank. Following good practice, Wai Yan's group then split their cleaned data into two separate parts -- most of it for training the model, and a smaller portion set aside and never shown to the model during training, reserved purely for testing afterward. Once trained, their model scored an impressive 96% accuracy on the training data itself, but only 71% on the held-back test data it had never seen -- a clear sign of overfitting, exactly as Week 32's own vocabulary described: the model had memorized quirks of the training examples rather than learning a pattern that generalized to new students. Wai Yan's group simplified their model and retrained it, this time achieving a more modest but far more trustworthy 82% on both training and test data, showing genuine generalization rather than memorization. Before considering the project finished, they ran one more check straight from Week 31: comparing the model's predictions across different groups of students to look for algorithmic bias, and confirmed it flagged students at a similar rate regardless of background rather than disproportionately targeting any one group. Wai Yan's teacher noted that this entire process -- clean, split, train, evaluate honestly, catch overfitting, retrain, and check for bias -- was exactly what separated a genuinely responsible predictive model from one that merely looked impressive on paper.",
