        passageEn:
            "At the end of the term, the teacher revealed the biggest challenge yet: a maze drawn on the grid, with the character starting in one corner and a trophy waiting at the other end. \"You will need everything we have learned this term,\" the teacher said, \"movement blocks, the right order, and a loop block, all working together.\"\n\nMoe Moe studied the maze path carefully. It went right three times in a straight line, then turned and went up once. \"I know exactly what to do,\" she said. \"First, I will use a repeat block set to 3 with a Move Right block inside it, for the straight part. Then I will snap a single Move Up block underneath it, because the path turns up at the very end.\"\n\nZaw Zaw almost snapped his Move Up block first, out of habit, but he stopped and looked at the maze again. \"Wait,\" he said, \"if I put Move Up before the repeat block, my character will climb the wall too early, before it has even gone right. The order has to match the path exactly.\" He rebuilt his program with the repeat block first, then Move Up last, just like Moe Moe.\n\nNay Chi pressed play on her own program, watching her character slide right, right, right -- thanks to her loop -- and then hop up onto the trophy square. All three friends cheered as their characters reached the trophy at exactly the same moment. \"Movement blocks, the right order, and a loop,\" said Nay Chi, \"put them all together, and you can solve any maze!\"",
