            "On a bright Tuesday afternoon, Ms. Thazin took the class outside with tablets for a photography lesson. 'Before you take a photo, think about what should be inside the frame,' she said. 'The frame is everything you can see through the camera screen.'\n\nMoe Moe pointed her tablet at Nay Chi and tapped the button quickly. When she looked at the photo, she laughed - a tall flagpole behind Nay Chi looked like it was sticking straight out of her head! Moe Moe moved a few steps to the side so the flagpole was no longer behind Nay Chi, then took the photo again. This time the background looked much better.\n\nNay Chi wanted to photograph the flowers in the school garden. At first, she stood far away, and the flowers looked tiny in the photo. She remembered what Ms. Thazin had said and walked closer until the flowers filled most of the frame. She also held the tablet with both hands so it would not shake.\n\nZaw Zaw tried to take a photo of a butterfly while running after it, but the photo came out blurry because his hands were shaking and moving. Moe Moe told him, 'Stand still, hold the tablet with both hands, and wait for the butterfly to land before you tap the button.' Zaw Zaw tried again, standing steady this time, and the new photo was sharp and clear.",
