            "Moe Moe, Nay Chi, and Zaw Zaw's school had a special Career Day. Parents came to talk about their jobs, and the children could not wait to hear how technology helped them every day. First, Nay Chi's mother, a nurse, showed a picture of an X-ray machine on her phone. She explained that the machine takes pictures inside a person's body, so doctors can see broken bones without cutting the skin at all.\n\nNext, Zaw Zaw's uncle, a farmer, spoke about his fields. He said that a smart watering system, controlled by a small computer, turned on by itself every morning to water his rice plants. He no longer needed to carry heavy buckets of water across the whole field, because the technology did that hard work for him.\n\nThen Moe Moe's aunt, who worked at a busy shop, showed the class a small barcode scanner. She beeped it over a toy box, and a price appeared instantly on a screen. \"This little scanner saves so much time,\" she said, \"so I can help more customers every single day.\"\n\nLast of all, a firefighter visited the class. He held up his radio and explained that when there is a fire, he presses one button and can talk to his whole team in seconds. \"Technology helps us work together quickly, so we can keep everyone safe,\" he said. Moe Moe, Nay Chi, and Zaw Zaw agreed that technology was helping people in more jobs than they had ever imagined.",
