            "During a class group project shared through an online document, Thidar noticed that one classmate kept writing unkind comments about another student's ideas, calling them silly and telling him to stop typing. At first, Thidar wasn't sure what to do -- the comments weren't aimed at her, and she worried that speaking up might cause an argument. But she remembered what her teacher had taught the class: someone who sees unkindness and says nothing is called a bystander, while someone who speaks up or gets help is called an upstander. Thidar decided to be an upstander. She quietly told her teacher what was happening in the document, rather than arguing with the unkind classmate herself. Her teacher thanked her for reporting it and spoke privately with the student who had written the unkind comments, reminding him that words typed online could hurt just as much as words spoken out loud. The classmate later apologized, and the group finished their project together peacefully. Thidar felt proud that she had chosen to help rather than simply watch, even though speaking up had felt a little scary at first.",
