            "For a class project about how technology changes over time, Kaung Sett asked his grandmother what computers were like when she was young. She laughed and told him that the very first computers she had heard about were so large they filled entire rooms, with technicians walking between rows of blinking lights and switches just to operate them. When she started working many years later, computers had shrunk down to the size of a desk, but they were still slow, and she remembered waiting several minutes just for a single document to open. Kaung Sett found this hard to imagine, since his family's tablet could open dozens of documents almost instantly and fit easily inside his school bag. Together, they drew a simple timeline on paper, marking room-sized computers first, then desk-sized computers, then laptops, and finally the small, fast tablets and phones Kaung Sett used today. His grandmother explained that this rapid change had not stopped, and that the computers Kaung Sett would use as an adult would likely be even smaller, faster, and more powerful than anything either of them could imagine right now.",
